gb: add the Switch Online Game Boy and Game Boy Pocket palettes

mGBA's libretro core has no way to load a palette from a file: the
`mgba_gb_colors` option is a list of names, filled at runtime from the
preset table in src/gb/overrides.c, and `_updateGbPal` looks the chosen
name up in that same table. A palette that is not in the table cannot be
selected by any frontend, so adding two means adding two rows.

The colours are the four-shade sets the Game Boy app on Nintendo Switch
Online uses for its original Game Boy and Game Boy Pocket looks,
as catalogued on Lospec ("DMG-NSO", "GBP-NSO", by Retronika;
a fan transcription, not a Nintendo publication):

  NSO DMG     #8cad28 #6c9421 #426b29 #214231
  NSO Pocket  #b5c69c #8d9c7b #637251 #303820

Presets hold 5 bits a channel, so each shade is rounded to the nearest
5-bit value; expanded back to 8 bits every channel lands within one
level of the source colour. Both use UNIFORM_PAL, the same shade set for
background and both sprite palettes, like the plain DMG presets above
them.

They sit directly after "GB Light" so the monochrome presets stay
together. That moves every later preset two places down, which matters
only to a frontend that stores a position instead of a name.

Applies to libretro/mgba at e31759b24e7a4e3899285ff720d7b573ac328ae7.

diff --git a/src/gb/overrides.c b/src/gb/overrides.c
index 04f90c3..fd897f3 100644
--- a/src/gb/overrides.c
+++ b/src/gb/overrides.c
@@ -707,6 +707,8 @@ static const struct GBColorPreset _colorPresets[] = {
 	{ "DMG Green", UNIFORM_PAL(0x2691, 0x19A9, 0x1105, 0x04A3), },
 	{ "GB Pocket", UNIFORM_PAL(0x52D4, 0x4270, 0x2989, 0x10A3), },
 	{ "GB Light", UNIFORM_PAL(0x7FCF, 0x738B, 0x56C3, 0x39E0), },
+	{ "NSO DMG", UNIFORM_PAL(0x16B1, 0x124D, 0x15A8, 0x1904), },
+	{ "NSO Pocket", UNIFORM_PAL(0x4F16, 0x3E71, 0x29CC, 0x10E6), },
 	{ "GBC Brown ↑", PALETTE(0, 0, 0), },
 	{ "GBC Red ↑A", PALETTE(4, 3, 28), },
 	{ "GBC Dark Brown ↑B", PALETTE(1, 0, 0), },
